---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { repoFile, SITE } from '../../lib/site';
---

<LegalLayout title="Licences" description="What the AGPL-3.0 server licence and the MIT SDK licence mean for you, in plain words." og="legal" updated="12 November 2026">
  <p>
    {SITE.name} uses two licences on purpose: a copyleft licence for the server, so improvements to the engine stay open, and a
    permissive one for the code you put in your own apps. This page explains them in plain words. The licence texts are what count.
  </p>

  <h2>The server: AGPL-3.0</h2>
  <p>
    The API, worker, dashboard and engine packages are licensed under the
    <a href={repoFile('LICENSE')}>GNU Affero General Public License v3.0</a>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You can run it for any purpose, including inside a commercial product, at no cost.</li>
    <li>You can read, change and share the code.</li>
    <li>
      If you change the server and let people use your changed version over a network, you must offer them the source code of your
      changes, under the same licence.
    </li>
    <li>Running it unchanged, or connecting your app to it through its API, does not on its own make your app's code subject to the AGPL.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The SDK: MIT</h2>
  <p>
    The TypeScript SDK, <code>@gami.fied/sdk</code>, is licensed under the <a href={repoFile('packages/sdk/LICENSE')}>MIT License</a>.
    You can use it in any app, open or closed source, and you only need to keep its copyright notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Future editions</h2>
  <p>
    Paid Cloud and Enterprise editions are planned. Enterprise-only features will live in their own folder with their own licence, and
    the engine in the open-source edition will not be cut down.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing</h2>
  <p>
    Contributions are welcome. How contributions are licensed is described in the repository before outside contributions are accepted.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
